KEYS TO SOME COMMON GENERA OF MOULDS


1,2 $ Spores 1-celled
1,12 $ Spores with more than one cell 
1,12,n$Usually this is obvious. Occasionally spores may have darkened areas resembling septa, but these will not be visible in optical section when you focus on the centre of the spore.
2,3 $ Colonies, spores, and other tissues colourless or brightly coloured
2,8 $ Colonies, spores, and/or other tissues dark coloured $ 8The dark colour is either brown or black. The best indication of dark colours comes from looking at the culture itself, either directly or with a dissecting microscope. Under the compound microscope many brown structures will appear nearly colourless.
3,4 $ Spores produced in chains 
3,4,n$Often chains of spores break apart thoroughly when placed in a water mount. In many species of Aspergillus and Penicillium a few spores will remain together in a group, so that you can assume that they were originally in chains. On the other hand, species of Cladosporium produce chains of spores that completely disassociate on contact and leave no clue about their original orientation. The easiest way to check this is to examine the colony under the 10X objective of a compound microscope, being careful not to get spores on the lens.
3,6 $ Spores not produced in chains
4,0 $ Conidiophores with a swollen head or vesicle bearing bottle-shaped phialides $ Aspergillus
4,5 $ Conidiophores not swollen at apex
5,0 $ Spores in unbranched chains, borne from clusters of cylindrical to bottle-shaped phialides; colonies usually green $ PenicilliumCompare with Paecilomyces (group II), Gliocladium (group III), and Scopulariopsis (group III).
5,0 $ Spores borne in branching chains from undifferentiated conidiophores; colonies often very fast growing and pink $ Chrysonilia
6,0 $ Spores borne in a sporangium with a columella; often with only the columella evident as a swollen hyphal tip; hyphae not septate $ MucorCompare Rhizopus (group I), Mortierella (group II), Absidia, Circinella (group V), and Zygorhynchus
6,7 $ Spores produced externally; hyphae septate
7,0 $ Conidiophores well-developed and usually with a central axis; very fast growing and with conidiophores usually produced in small cushions of hyphae; often green $ TrichodermaCompare with Verticillium (group II) and Gliocladium (group III)
7,0 $ Conidiophores poorly developed or lacking; phialides produced singly along the vegetative hyphae; hyphae often aggregated into "ropes"; seldom or never green $ AcremoniumCompare with Verticillium (group II), Sporothrix (group IV), and Phialophora (group IV)
8,9 $ Spores in chains, produced externally
8,10 $ Spores not in chains, produced inside sporangia or fruiting bodies (pycnidia)
9,0 $ Conidiophores with a swollen head or vesicle bearing bottle-shaped phialides; conidial chains unbranched $ Aspergillus
9,0 $ Conidiophores lacking a swollen apex; spore chains often branched; spores often both 1- and 2-celled $ Cladosporium
10,0 $ Spores produced inside a fruiting body (pycnidium) with a cellular wall; hyphae septate $ PhomaCompare Pyrenochaeta (group IV) and Microsphaeropsis (group IV) and also be sure that asci are not present at a very early stage
10,11 $ Spores produced within a sporangium with a columella, often with only the columella evident as a swollen hyphal tip; hyphae not septate
11,0 $ Sporangiophores with rhizoids (branched "roots") at base $ RhizopusCompare with Absidia (figure 2B)
11,0 $ Sporangiophores lacking rhizoids $ MucorCompare with Mortierella (group II), Absidia (figure 2B), Circinella (group V), and Zygorhynchus (figure 2C)
12,13 $ Spores with transverse septa only
12,14 $ Spores with both transverse and vertical septa
13,0 $ Spores dark, produced in branched chains $ Cladosporium
13,0 $ Spores colourless or brightly coloured, mostly with more than two cells, often canoe-shaped, usually produced in slimy masses; colonies often pink $ FusariumCompare Cylindrocarpon (not treated here), Candelabrella, Monacrosporium (all group III), and Trichophyton (group V)
14,0 $ Spores usually in chains, usually club-shaped; colonies grey to brown $ AlternariaCompare Ulocladium and Stemphylium (group II)
14,0 $ Spores in clusters but not in chains, usually spherical; colonies often (but not always) bright orange or yellow and purplish in reverse $ Epicoccum
